<div><p>Gunn told CL that officers in two St. Petersburg police cruisers stopped by to question him for about 10–15 minutes before driving off. Footage shared with CL appears to show two cruisers stopped in the road beside Gunn as the driver taking the video shouts: “Yo, what’s your name? I’ll come bail you out!”</p><p>Flock cameras are run by a private company and have been in St. Petersburg since 2022. The St. Petersburg Police Department has said that the cameras help solve crimes. Civil liberties organizations and bipartisan lawmakers say Flock cameras are an unprecedented mass surveillance tool that could change the landscape of personal freedom nationwide.</p><p>Like any security camera, Flock cameras collect video evidence constantly. Unlike other cameras, they automatically read each license plate, collect every detail about a scene through AI and put it all in a searchable database.</p><p>A police officer can search 100,000 cameras nationwide to see if a fugitive has passed any Flock cameras, or get an alert when they do. They can also search for identifying features like make, model, color or even unique dents and bumper stickers.</p><p>Police can also use the cameras to surveil protestors and stalk ex-lovers—or strangers they find attractive.</p><p>The cameras have raised questions on constitutionality and have led to wrongful arrests across the county.</p></div>



