ent/uploads/2026/07/CarlGunnTalking-1024x768.jpg?w=370&amp;ssl=1 370w" sizes="(max-width: 780px) 100vw, 780px"><figcaption>Carl Gunn in St. Petersburg, Florida on July 25, 2026. Photo by Valerie Smith.  <span><span>Credit:</span> Valerie Smith / Creative Loafing Tampa Bay</span></figcaption></figure>



<div><p>“We’re private citizens; we don’t need this stuff,” Gunn told CL. “And they’re reporting now that the Chinese have already picked up all of our voter registrations. They’re picking up more information on us, on how we live, and you know there’s a reason for it.”</p><p>Experts have said that obtaining voter data is fairly common and that it doesn’t necessarily indicate a breach of voting systems or election interference.</p><p>Gunn’s protest was inspired in part by a trend where people damage Flock cameras in an effort to reduce surveillance in their cities. Instead, he wanted to find a legal, nondestructive way to protest and inhibit Flock. “They don’t own the air,” Gunn told CL.</p></div>



