<p>St. Petersburg already has around 50 Flock cameras. <a href="https://maps.deflock.org/?lat=27.7712&amp;lng=-82.6340&amp;zoom=11.00">The DeFlock website</a> has a map showing their locations, and <a href="https://dontgetflocked.com/">the FlockHopper app</a> can be used to navigate without passing any of them. Law enforcement across the country—including immigration agents—may request access to the data.</p>



<p>One camera covers the entry to the St. Pete Pier. Another watches Wildwood Park and a youth rec center in south St. Pete. Nine Flock cameras blanket a retail complex surrounding Lowes and Home Depot.</p>



<div><p>According to records loaded into Flock monitoring website Have I Been Flocked, St. Pete police have used the cameras to search for suspects in&nbsp; federal investigations targeting noncitizens, vandalism cases, and, in one instance, littering.</p><p>“In a democracy, the government shouldn’t be watching its citizens all the time just in case we do something wrong,” ACLU Senior Policy Analyst Jay Stanley said in 2025. “It’s one thing if a police officer out on a street sees something suspicious in public and reacts. But this is an entirely different matter.”</p><p>Flock, founded in 2017, “surpassed $300 million in annual recurring revenue in early 2025, a 70 percent increase year over year,” according to the TDG wealth management company.</p><p>“The Flock company, they’re doing this for profit,” Gunn told CL. “We didn’t give them permission to do it on us. If they want to do it for profit, I want my paycheck.”</p></div>



